WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.804
Welcome to Module 1 of the
Provider Notices learning path:

2
00:00:03.804 --> 00:00:05.837
why provider notices go unread.

3
00:00:05.837 --> 00:00:11.316
Every cloud provider tells the agency, in
writing and ahead of time, what it will

4
00:00:11.316 --> 00:00:16.625
change, retire or patch in the agency's
own tenant, account, tenancy or project.

5
00:00:16.625 --> 00:00:21.475
The notices land in a portal or one
person's inbox, and nobody owns them.

6
00:00:21.475 --> 00:00:26.103
This path teaches you to read each
provider's notices from its own record,

7
00:00:26.103 --> 00:00:29.574
hold every notice as one
record, give the ones that need

8
00:00:29.574 --> 00:00:32.813
action an owner and a due
date, and keep the evidence.

9
00:00:32.813 --> 00:00:37.020
It follows the seven gates of the
provider notices implementation guide,

10
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.262
with the developer workbook
Building Provider Notices.

11
00:00:40.262 --> 00:00:42.713
This first module comes before the gates.

12
00:00:42.713 --> 00:00:46.387
It takes about forty-five
minutes, with a lab on fixtures.

13
00:00:46.387 --> 00:00:52.240
The code is illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

14
00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:58.178
Why do provider notices go unread?

15
00:00:58.178 --> 00:00:59.665
Providers don't hide them.

16
00:00:59.665 --> 00:01:01.821
Azure shows them in Service Health,

17
00:01:01.821 --> 00:01:05.837
but nobody is told unless someone
created a Service Health alert,

18
00:01:05.837 --> 00:01:09.034
and Microsoft's own F A Q
gives a rule that left out the

19
00:01:09.034 --> 00:01:12.603
affected subscription as a
reason an alert never arrived.

20
00:01:12.603 --> 00:01:17.932
Microsoft 365 posts them in the Message
center, where each administrator's view

21
00:01:17.932 --> 00:01:23.341
is their own, and the weekly digest goes
to each administrator's own primary email.

22
00:01:23.341 --> 00:01:27.240
Oracle Cloud Infrastructure
emails the tenancy administrator.

23
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.390
AWS writes to the root user's address
and up to three alternate contacts.

24
00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:35.540
And Google Cloud writes to
its Essential Contacts.

25
00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:39.852
Every one of those channels ends at
a person or a page, not a queue.

26
00:01:39.852 --> 00:01:44.566
The notice carries no owner, no due date
and no way to show it was dealt with.

27
00:01:44.566 --> 00:01:47.415
Attackers imitate provider email, too.

28
00:01:47.415 --> 00:01:51.332
And a retirement announced a year
ahead is easy to agree with,

29
00:01:51.332 --> 00:01:53.340
and easy to miss on the day.

30
00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:00.003
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

31
00:02:00.003 --> 00:02:04.331
You'll explain why each provider's
notices end at a person or a page,

32
00:02:04.331 --> 00:02:08.340
and why email stays an early
warning, not the system of record.

33
00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:11.978
You'll name the six kinds of
notice, and who owns each one.

34
00:02:11.978 --> 00:02:13.676
You'll read the record's fields,

35
00:02:13.676 --> 00:02:17.478
and tell a provider's own action date
from one the kit's rule inferred.

36
00:02:17.478 --> 00:02:21.753
And you'll show that a second read of
the same notices changes no fingerprint.

37
00:02:21.753 --> 00:02:25.052
There's nothing to take first:
this is the first module.

38
00:02:25.052 --> 00:02:26.947
If you have time, read the intake,

39
00:02:26.947 --> 00:02:31.976
triage and routing chapter of Compliance
Automation and Day-Two Operations, which

40
00:02:31.976 --> 00:02:36.714
routes Microsoft's service health email
today and never acts on an email alone.

41
00:02:36.714 --> 00:02:42.903
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1 or later with
Pester 5, and a copy of the repository.

42
00:02:42.903 --> 00:02:44.190
No tenant.

43
00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.378
Each provider names its
notices differently.

44
00:02:50.378 --> 00:02:52.748
The program sorts them into six kinds,

45
00:02:52.748 --> 00:02:57.414
because the kind decides who owns the
notice and what it asks of the agency.

46
00:02:57.414 --> 00:03:00.941
Maintenance: work is scheduled
that may affect availability,

47
00:03:00.941 --> 00:03:04.190
so check the window against
the systems that depend on it.

48
00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:08.897
Retirement: a feature, version or
resource type is ending, with a date,

49
00:03:08.897 --> 00:03:12.427
so plan and approve the
migration before that date,

50
00:03:12.427 --> 00:03:16.378
and review the contingency plan
if a system depends on it.

51
00:03:16.378 --> 00:03:20.985
Advisory: information or a
recommendation, often with no deadline;

52
00:03:20.985 --> 00:03:24.503
decide whether it applies,
and record the decision.

53
00:03:24.503 --> 00:03:29.390
Security: assess the exposure and act
within the security response window.

54
00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:32.660
Change: treat it as a proposed
change to the system,

55
00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:36.090
with an impact analysis and
then the change process.

56
00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:38.503
And incident: something is broken now.

57
00:03:38.503 --> 00:03:40.827
Not every provider has every kind.

58
00:03:40.827 --> 00:03:45.122
Google Cloud's Personalized Service
Health reports incidents only:

59
00:03:45.122 --> 00:03:49.700
its event category has one value,
for service outage or degradation.

60
00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:54.813
Who owns each kind?

61
00:03:54.813 --> 00:03:58.073
The Compliance Automation
and Day-Two Operations

62
00:03:58.073 --> 00:04:01.100
book uses the roles it
already has, and adds none.

63
00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:04.219
An incident belongs to the
service desk supervisor,

64
00:04:04.219 --> 00:04:06.250
as service health is routed today.

65
00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:11.256
Maintenance belongs to the platform
owner of the affected platform:

66
00:04:11.256 --> 00:04:16.525
the cloud platform owner for Azure, AWS,
Google Cloud and each O C I tenancy,

67
00:04:16.525 --> 00:04:19.950
and the collaboration
owner for Microsoft 365.

68
00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:24.552
A retirement belongs to the system owner
of each affected system, who decides

69
00:04:24.552 --> 00:04:28.938
the schedule, and the platform owner
does the work through a normal change.

70
00:04:28.938 --> 00:04:33.328
A change is proposed by the platform
owner and authorized by the

71
00:04:33.328 --> 00:04:37.801
change advisory board, after the
ISSO's security impact analysis.

72
00:04:37.801 --> 00:04:41.137
Security notices belong
to security operations.

73
00:04:41.137 --> 00:04:45.549
And an advisory belongs to the platform
owner of the platform it names.

74
00:04:45.549 --> 00:04:49.338
Two decisions have no owner yet,
and the book doesn't invent one:

75
00:04:49.338 --> 00:04:52.458
who receives each provider's
notices at the source,

76
00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:57.560
and who reviews the contingency plan when
a notice names a system's resources.

77
00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:03.735
Here's the loop the rest
of the path builds.

78
00:05:03.735 --> 00:05:06.395
Collect from the
provider, not the mailbox:

79
00:05:06.395 --> 00:05:10.575
a read-only identity reads each
provider's notices at least weekly,

80
00:05:10.575 --> 00:05:15.135
through the provider's own interface,
and each notice becomes one record.

81
00:05:15.135 --> 00:05:19.318
Route by rules, in order: security
advisories, then retirements,

82
00:05:19.318 --> 00:05:22.776
then maintenance that names
the agency's resources,

83
00:05:22.776 --> 00:05:28.085
then open incidents that name them, then
anything else marked as needing action.

84
00:05:28.085 --> 00:05:32.997
The first match wins, and a notice that
matches none goes to the weekly digest.

85
00:05:32.997 --> 00:05:37.769
A notice that needs action becomes a
typed task in the I T S M front door,

86
00:05:37.769 --> 00:05:42.122
not a second queue, with an owner,
a severity and response window,

87
00:05:42.122 --> 00:05:45.135
and the provider's own
date kept separately.

88
00:05:45.135 --> 00:05:47.481
It's keyed to the provider's notice I D,

89
00:05:47.481 --> 00:05:50.610
so an unchanged notice
never makes a second task.

90
00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:54.599
And a task closes only on the
re-read: when the next collection

91
00:05:54.599 --> 00:05:59.147
shows the notice closed, or the
agency's resources no longer affected.

92
00:05:59.147 --> 00:06:01.899
A retirement can close
on the provider's side

93
00:06:01.899 --> 00:06:05.230
while the agency still
runs the retired version.

94
00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:13.423
Unit 2 is the answer: one record
for every provider's notice,

95
00:06:13.423 --> 00:06:19.255
called f m p provider notice, version
one, defined by a JSON schema in the kit.

96
00:06:19.255 --> 00:06:22.792
The notice I D is the provider
and the provider's own I D,

97
00:06:22.792 --> 00:06:25.605
and it's the key the
router de-duplicates on.

98
00:06:25.605 --> 00:06:29.717
The kind is one of the six, and
the status is open or closed.

99
00:06:29.717 --> 00:06:33.140
The window is the impact
window, always in U T C.

100
00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:37.580
Services, regions and resources
are what the notice names.

101
00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:41.990
Then three fields that matter
most: whether action is required,

102
00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:45.468
by when, and the action basis,
which says who said so.

103
00:06:45.468 --> 00:06:48.761
Either the provider said it,
in a field the record names,

104
00:06:48.761 --> 00:06:52.493
or a rule of the kit's inferred
it, and the record states the rule.

105
00:06:52.493 --> 00:06:55.542
First seen is when the
kit first read the notice.

106
00:06:55.542 --> 00:06:58.822
And the fingerprint is
a SHA-256 of the record

107
00:06:58.822 --> 00:07:02.279
without first seen and
the fingerprint itself.

108
00:07:02.279 --> 00:07:04.764
So a provider's edit
changes the fingerprint,

109
00:07:04.764 --> 00:07:07.718
and reading the same notice
again a week later doesn't.

110
00:07:07.718 --> 00:07:11.850
That's how the router tells a changed
notice from one it has already seen.

111
00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:18.041
The guide compares the five providers,

112
00:07:18.041 --> 00:07:21.625
and one row matters most here:
who says action is required.

113
00:07:21.625 --> 00:07:25.339
Azure's Service Health, read
through Azure Resource Graph,

114
00:07:25.339 --> 00:07:27.737
has no field the kit reads that says so.

115
00:07:27.737 --> 00:07:32.517
So the kit applies a stated rule:
a retirement is action required,

116
00:07:32.517 --> 00:07:36.484
due at the advisory's end
time, and a security advisory

117
00:07:36.484 --> 00:07:40.451
is action required with no
date, for the ISSO to review.

118
00:07:40.451 --> 00:07:44.512
Microsoft 365 has its act-by date field.

119
00:07:44.512 --> 00:07:47.274
Oracle Cloud Infrastructure has its action

120
00:07:47.274 --> 00:07:49.887
required type and action required by date.

121
00:07:49.887 --> 00:07:52.737
AWS has its actionability field.

122
00:07:52.737 --> 00:07:56.434
And Google Cloud's Personalized
Service Health doesn't say,

123
00:07:56.434 --> 00:08:01.413
and has no planned maintenance at all: its
event category has one value, incident.

124
00:08:01.413 --> 00:08:03.517
Wherever the provider doesn't say,

125
00:08:03.517 --> 00:08:07.350
the record's action basis begins
with rule, and states the rule.

126
00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:11.150
Nobody mistakes the kit's inference
for the provider's own word.

127
00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:15.625
Try it.

128
00:08:15.625 --> 00:08:19.438
Lab 1 runs in the provider
notices folder of the examples,

129
00:08:19.438 --> 00:08:24.550
in PowerShell 7, against two fictional
weeks of notices from every provider.

130
00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:27.563
Nothing signs in to anything
or sends a request.

131
00:08:27.563 --> 00:08:32.005
Step one imports the kit's module
and collects every provider

132
00:08:32.005 --> 00:08:35.663
from the day-one fixtures,
as of November ninth, 2026.

133
00:08:35.663 --> 00:08:40.029
Five from Azure, six from
Microsoft 365 in the G C C cloud,

134
00:08:40.029 --> 00:08:46.337
four from Oracle Cloud Infrastructure,
five from AWS and two from Google Cloud.

135
00:08:46.337 --> 00:08:50.190
Twenty-two notices in all,
each valid against the schema,

136
00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:55.801
written as JSON and as a spreadsheet file,
with the raw responses and a manifest.

137
00:08:55.801 --> 00:08:57.225
Notice the cloud column.

138
00:08:57.225 --> 00:09:01.587
Every provider names its own cloud in
the settings, and there is no default.

139
00:09:01.587 --> 00:09:05.090
Five providers, five shapes, one list.

140
00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:14.509
Step two counts the notices by kind, across
providers: five advisories, two changes,

141
00:09:14.509 --> 00:09:20.428
six incidents, three maintenance windows,
four retirements and two security notices.

142
00:09:20.428 --> 00:09:24.663
The kind decides the owner, so the
four retirements go to each affected

143
00:09:24.663 --> 00:09:28.752
system's owner, and the two security
notices to security operations.

144
00:09:28.752 --> 00:09:31.940
Step three asks who said
each one needs action.

145
00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:33.240
Eight notices do.

146
00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:38.214
Four times the provider said so:
two from AWS's actionability field,

147
00:09:38.214 --> 00:09:44.002
one from Microsoft 365's act-by date, and
one from Oracle's action required type.

148
00:09:44.002 --> 00:09:46.007
And four times a kit rule did:

149
00:09:46.007 --> 00:09:51.500
an AWS scheduled change whose code names
a retirement, the Azure retirement,

150
00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:56.993
due at the advisory's end, the Azure
security advisory, which the ISSO reviews,

151
00:09:56.993 --> 00:10:01.440
and a Microsoft 365 post in the
prevent or fix issues category.

152
00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:03.728
The record says which, every time.

153
00:10:03.728 --> 00:10:08.950
And where the provider gave no date,
the kit makes none up: it says no date.

154
00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:15.687
Step four reads the
Azure retirement in full.

155
00:10:15.687 --> 00:10:19.925
Its notice I D is azure,
R T five J six K L.

156
00:10:19.925 --> 00:10:22.866
It's due on September thirtieth, 2027,

157
00:10:22.866 --> 00:10:26.200
by the kit's rule, at
the advisory's end time.

158
00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:32.150
It names one public I P address,
and its fingerprint is a SHA-256.

159
00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:35.015
Step five reads the same
fixtures a day later,

160
00:10:35.015 --> 00:10:39.275
as of November tenth, and compares
the fingerprints of the two runs.

161
00:10:39.275 --> 00:10:41.412
The same twenty-two notices come back,

162
00:10:41.412 --> 00:10:44.238
and the count of fingerprints
that differ is zero.

163
00:10:44.238 --> 00:10:48.287
A second read changes first seen
in the run, but no fingerprint,

164
00:10:48.287 --> 00:10:52.575
so the router in Module 7 sees
nothing new and makes no second task.

165
00:10:52.575 --> 00:10:56.495
Step six runs the record's
tests, for the notice record,

166
00:10:56.495 --> 00:11:00.950
the fingerprint, and times and
text: seventeen pass, none fail.

167
00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:03.160
Then delete the output folder.

168
00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:09.147
This module has no Lab 2 of its own,

169
00:11:09.147 --> 00:11:12.710
because the record is the
same in a test environment.

170
00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:15.667
Each provider's Lab 2, in Modules 2 to 6,

171
00:11:15.667 --> 00:11:21.285
fills it from a test subscription,
tenant, tenancy, account or project.

172
00:11:21.285 --> 00:11:23.997
Never production, and only fictional data.

173
00:11:23.997 --> 00:11:26.174
Before you start them, do one thing:

174
00:11:26.174 --> 00:11:29.284
list who receives each
provider's notices today.

175
00:11:29.284 --> 00:11:33.187
The guide's prerequisites start with
that decision, and the deployment

176
00:11:33.187 --> 00:11:37.685
runbook's pre-flight checks that each
enabled provider names its notice contacts.

177
00:11:37.685 --> 00:11:39.997
And remember where the real work happens.

178
00:11:39.997 --> 00:11:42.511
The labs use the kit's fictional routing.

179
00:11:42.511 --> 00:11:45.880
The agency's own routing,
settings files, notices,

180
00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:50.934
digests and evidence are written at
work, from the kit's templates and its

181
00:11:50.934 --> 00:11:56.770
Copilot prompts in Microsoft 365 Copilot
Chat, and they stay on agency storage.

182
00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:01.907
The federal requirement.

183
00:12:01.907 --> 00:12:08.051
Every gate's collection supports NIST
special publication eight hundred fifty-three

184
00:12:08.051 --> 00:12:13.255
S A nine, part c, monitoring external
service providers on an ongoing basis,

185
00:12:13.255 --> 00:12:17.521
and S I five, part a, receiving
security alerts and advisories.

186
00:12:17.521 --> 00:12:22.408
The record supports S I five and
C A seven, continuous monitoring.

187
00:12:22.408 --> 00:12:26.795
The evidence is the raw responses,
the notices and the manifest,

188
00:12:26.795 --> 00:12:32.447
which lists every call with its role; the
notices as one record each, valid against

189
00:12:32.447 --> 00:12:37.846
the schema; and a re-read that changes no
fingerprint, so a notice is routed once.

190
00:12:37.846 --> 00:12:40.358
The record is evidence for these controls.

191
00:12:40.358 --> 00:12:44.797
Your organization still sets their
parameters, policy and procedures,

192
00:12:44.797 --> 00:12:48.033
and the assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

193
00:12:48.033 --> 00:12:52.670
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers.

194
00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:57.179
The agency lines this evidence up
with what its providers report;

195
00:12:57.179 --> 00:12:59.183
it doesn't report K S I's itself.

196
00:12:59.183 --> 00:13:02.790
Which file supports which
indicator is the program's mapping.

197
00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:08.515
Check yourself with two questions.

198
00:13:08.515 --> 00:13:13.515
First: why does the program read each
provider's A P I instead of its email?

199
00:13:13.515 --> 00:13:14.554
The answer is b.

200
00:13:14.554 --> 00:13:17.412
Email reaches a person who may have left,

201
00:13:17.412 --> 00:13:20.790
carries no owner or due
date, and can be imitated.

202
00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:25.803
The provider's own record is the source of
truth, and email stays an early warning.

203
00:13:25.803 --> 00:13:29.789
Second: the Azure retirement's
action basis starts with rule.

204
00:13:29.789 --> 00:13:30.890
What does that mean?

205
00:13:30.890 --> 00:13:32.115
The answer is b.

206
00:13:32.115 --> 00:13:35.666
The kit inferred that action
is required by a stated rule,

207
00:13:35.666 --> 00:13:38.915
because Azure has no field
the kit reads that says so.

208
00:13:38.915 --> 00:13:40.960
Microsoft didn't set that date;

209
00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:45.215
the kit's rule took the advisory's
end time, and the record says so.

210
00:13:45.215 --> 00:13:47.642
The module page has three more questions,

211
00:13:47.642 --> 00:13:50.490
each linked to the
section that answers it.

212
00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:55.065
To sum up.

213
00:13:55.065 --> 00:13:59.874
You collected twenty-two fictional
notices from five providers into one list,

214
00:13:59.874 --> 00:14:04.165
counted them by the six kinds, saw
eight that need action and who said so,

215
00:14:04.165 --> 00:14:07.790
and showed that reading them
again changes no fingerprint.

216
00:14:07.790 --> 00:14:13.295
The evidence an ISSO keeps from this module's
pattern is each run's raw responses,

217
00:14:13.295 --> 00:14:17.715
the notices as JSON and as a
spreadsheet file, and the manifest

218
00:14:17.715 --> 00:14:22.302
that lists every call with its role
and hashes every file, all dated.

219
00:14:22.302 --> 00:14:26.816
Modules 2 to 6 produce it for each
provider, and Module 8 chains it.

220
00:14:26.816 --> 00:14:30.677
Next is Module 2: Azure
Service Health and alerts.

221
00:14:30.677 --> 00:14:35.367
That's Gate 1, with the workbook's first
chapter: the Azure reader, the elevated

222
00:14:35.367 --> 00:14:37.303
role security advisories need,

223
00:14:37.303 --> 00:14:40.870
and Service Health alerts at
scale through Azure Policy.
